# How far back to look in production logs. Widened 24h -> 7d on 2026-09-10:
# the 24h window held no `sync-complete` at all, so the four phase-2 sync
# signals had no denominator and said nothing either way. Narrow it again once
# a sync has been observed.
#
# WIDENED 7d -> 14d on 2026-09-19, and the reason is the deploy boundary. B1
# (the rewritten topic sidecar: streaming, no pandas, counts from DuckDB) went
# live 2026-09-11 15:20 UTC. A 7d window read on 09-19 starts at 09-12, so it
# sits entirely AFTER that boundary and cannot tell "the new sidecar has never
# written" from "it wrote before the window opened" — the two look identical
# and only one of them is a fault. 14d reaches 09-05, which carries the whole
# post-deploy period AND a pre-deploy stretch to contrast it against:
# `delta write complete` is a line the OLD sidecar could not emit, so finding
# it only after 09-11 15:20 is what proves the new image shipped with its venv.
# It also clears the first post-B1 Sunday 03:00 maintenance (2026-09-13) with
# margin instead of by two days — at 7d that run falls out of the window on
# 09-21, and nothing has ever compacted or vacuumed a topic table before it.
#
# How far back to look in production logs. An OPTIONAL second value line
# `tenant <id>` narrows every signature to lines carrying that tenantId —
# the log filter that did not exist anywhere until 2026-09-05 (assessment
# 6-2): "find this customer's errors" was a container-wide read. A
# `request <id>` line does the same for one correlation id (6-1): the id an
# error shows on /admin/ops finds the request, its job and the sync worker's
# lines in one query.
#
#   tenant 42
#   request 8ec98557-14e2-4ee3-9c6b-4a7f56a08c69

#
#   <number>h  or  <number>d   — e.g. 24h, 7d. Log Analytics keeps 30 days.
#
# Editing this file runs a READ-ONLY query against the Log Analytics workspace
# and reports whether a short list of known failure signatures appeared. It
# changes nothing, so re-run it as often as you like — the point is that it is
# cheap enough to actually be used.
#
# Every one of these signatures is something CLAUDE.md already tells the next
# person to "watch for", and none of them has ever been looked at, because
# looking meant having Azure credentials on a laptop. That is the gap this
# closes: the signal a change was supposed to produce is now readable from a
# workflow log by anyone on the team.
#
# What it looks for:
#
#   grant-missing     "42501" / insufficient_privilege / permission denied for
#                     — a table the app role was never granted. THE residual
#                     risk of the database role flip: verification exercised
#                     login, not every code path.
#                     THE SQLSTATE IS MATCHED QUOTED, and that is not fussiness:
#                     the bare form matched an epoch-millisecond TIMESTAMP. The
#                     2026-09-10 run reported two grant-missing hits whose
#                     example line was a 200 on /api/ping, because
#                     `"time":1789042501257` contains 42501. Roughly one line in
#                     ten thousand does, so this BAD-labelled signal fired
#                     steadily on nothing — which is how a reader teaches people
#                     to ignore the one signature that says the role flip broke
#                     something. pg reports the code as a string, so `"42501"`
#                     keeps every real hit and drops every timestamp.
#   rls-write-denied  a policy refusing an INSERT/UPDATE. Fail-closed, which is
#                     the right direction, but it means a user hit a wall.
#                     TESTED BEFORE grant-missing, deliberately: Postgres uses
#                     SQLSTATE 42501 for BOTH a policy refusal and a missing
#                     GRANT, and case() takes the first branch that matches, so
#                     with the old order every RLS refusal was reported as a
#                     missing grant. Different cause, different fix — grant the
#                     table, versus carry the tenant context. The 2026-09-10
#                     run's 19 "grant-missing" hits were all the second kind.
#   ownership-refused the tenant ownership gate turning away traffic. A burst
#                     means it is rejecting legitimate requests — a graph entity
#                     whose Postgres mirror row is missing — not that someone is
#                     probing.
#   server-error      any request the API answered with a 5xx — matched on the
#                     literal 'request failed', which is what requestLogger
#                     emits at >=500 (4xx says 'request error'; the first
#                     version of this signature looked for the wrong one of
#                     the two and so could never have matched). Added 2026-08-29
#                     while chasing a console that refused its own operator:
#                     the frontend renders EVERY failure of that endpoint as
#                     "this page is not open to you", so a 500 and a refusal
#                     look identical from the screen. This is the signature
#                     that tells them apart — and more generally it is the
#                     closest thing to alerting this platform has.
#   runner-active     POSITIVE signal. The child-process query runner announcing
#                     itself once per boot. Its absence, with DUCKDB_RUNNER set
#                     to `child`, means it silently fell back in-process.
#   runner-degraded   the runner saying so explicitly.
#
#   brief-run         POSITIVE. `'morning brief run done'` — the 06:00 nightly
#                     job finished at all. This is the DENOMINATOR for the four
#                     lines below: without it, their absence means "the job
#                     never ran", not "it ran and found nothing to do", and
#                     those are completely different problems.
#   overnight-ok      POSITIVE. The overnight investigation concluded, so the
#                     answer to "why did this move?" was waiting on /home
#                     before anyone opened it. Shipped 2026-09-07 and, until
#                     the first of these lines appears, NEVER RUN ON REAL DATA.
#   overnight-skipped it ran and declined — either nothing breached a
#                     sensitivity threshold (the cost gate working: a quiet
#                     morning is meant to be free) or the reader is dormant.
#                     Not a fault. The log line says which.
#   overnight-failed  the picked entry's product was not queryable. Degrades to
#                     the pre-R2 behaviour — a fresh investigation when the user
#                     clicks — not to a broken page. A RUN of these is the
#                     signal; one is a bad night.
#
#   readability-*     The dashboard READABILITY gate (2026-09-09,
#                     services/widgetReadability.ts) — five lines, all emitted
#                     by routes/dashboards.ts at generation / refine / fix:
#                     `spec-fix` a chart fixed with no model call (pie → bar,
#                     count → format number); `to-model` a chart handed to the
#                     repair model with the exact rewrite; `repaired` the
#                     re-check confirmed the rewrite; `unresolved` STILL
#                     unreadable after the rewrite — shipped with a note on the
#                     card and "Fix with AI" (a run of these = the fix rule
#                     needs tightening); `repair-broke` the rewrite errored and
#                     the pre-repair version was kept (a prompt regression).
#                     Until the first `to-model` line appears the gate has never
#                     met a real model response. Read `repaired` against
#                     `to-model`: that ratio is the model's hit rate.
#
#   sync-complete     POSITIVE. `'sync complete'` — a source entity finished
#                     pulling. This is the DENOMINATOR for the four sync lines
#                     below, exactly as brief-run is for the overnight ones.
#                     Read its `mode:` field: `merge:<key>` is phase 2's
#                     anti-join merge, and seeing it land without an OOM on the
#                     largest entity is what phase 2 was waiting to observe.
#   sync-budget-stop  `'stopped at the time budget'` — an entity stopped CLEANLY
#                     before the orchestrator's ceiling instead of being killed
#                     by it (phase 2, B3). Expected, and it should be followed
#                     by sync-continuation. A stop with no continuation means
#                     the chain refused (cap reached, or a part that made no
#                     progress) and the run is partial.
#   sync-continuation `'continuation run queued'` — the follow-up run for
#                     exactly the unfinished entities, resuming from their
#                     checkpoints. A long streak for one connection means the
#                     ceiling is too tight for that table, not that resume is
#                     broken.
#   sync-tombstoned   the deleted-row check hid rows the source no longer has
#                     ('full re-sync finalised', or any line carrying a
#                     `"tombstoned"` field — reconcile emits it too). Small
#                     counts are the feature working. A large `tombstoned`
#                     beside a small `rowsTotal` means the source returned less
#                     than it holds and the read firewall is now hiding real
#                     data: reconcile again before trusting those tables.
#   sync-failed       `'sync run failed'` — the same string clarion-failed-syncs
#                     alerts on, covering failed AND partial runs.
#   delta-write       POSITIVE. `'delta write complete'` — the topic sidecar
#                     rewritten on 2026-09-10 (streaming, no pandas, counts from
#                     DuckDB) committed a Delta version. Its absence over a
#                     window with topic refreshes means the OLD sidecar still
#                     runs. Read `writeMode` and `countsMeasured`.
#   delta-maintenance `'delta maintenance done'` — the weekly compaction +
#                     vacuum over one tenant's topic tables (added the same day;
#                     nothing ever did either before). `-failed` is its BAD twin.
#   maintenance-run  POSITIVE, and the DENOMINATOR for the two above.
#                     `'warehouse maintenance start'` — the weekly sweep began
#                     (cron `0 3 * * 0`, jobs-worker). Added 2026-09-20 because
#                     run #15 could not answer the one question it was widened
#                     to answer. `maintainProductTables` returns EARLY and
#                     SILENTLY when no tenant holds a Delta topic table, so
#                     `delta-maintenance` alone cannot separate "the sweep never
#                     ran" from "it ran and had nothing to do". This line is
#                     logged before any Delta work, so the pair disambiguates:
#                     both present = working; this one alone = ran, nothing to
#                     maintain; this one ABSENT = the repeatable never fired.
#   maintenance-registered
#                     DIAGNOSTIC, added 2026-09-20 to find out WHY the sweep
#                     never fires. `'Weekly warehouse maintenance registered'`,
#                     logged at the END of registerWeeklyMaintenance — so one
#                     occurrence is one boot of the scheduler-owning process
#                     that removed the repeatable and re-added it. A HIGH count
#                     makes the remove-then-re-add churn real and measured
#                     rather than theorised. ABSENT would OVERTURN the
#                     inference that registration happens at all (that was
#                     drawn indirectly, from brief-run firing while
#                     registerDailyBrief is called immediately after it).
#   queues-configured DIAGNOSTIC, same day. `'WORKER_QUEUES'` — one line per
#                     process boot naming the queues that process serves. The
#                     direct evidence that the jobs-worker starts its workers
#                     at all: the example line's hostname says which container
#                     and its `queues` array says what it consumes. Preflight
#                     run 7 proved the ENV VAR lists warehouse-maintenance;
#                     this proves the PROCESS acted on it.
#   source-stale      the hourly freshness sweep (wave B 7-1) finding a source
#                     whose schedule says data should have landed and it has
#                     not. Added 2026-09-10 because it is the ONE signal that
#                     separates "nobody configured a sync" from "syncs are
#                     configured and not running" — and run #10 proved the
#                     sync-worker job has not executed in seven days, which is
#                     only a fault if something was meant to trigger it.
#
# THE FIVE ABOVE ARE WHY THIS READER NOW COVERS THE SYNC WORKER. It is a
# Container Apps JOB, and until 2026-09-10 the query filtered on two Container
# APP names — so `sync complete`, `stopped at the time budget` and the
# tombstone counts, all of which are written by the worker's own stdout, could
# never have matched. (The orchestrator relays worker lines only into a 10 KB
# `logExcerpt` on the run row; they do not pass through the API's logger.) The
# job's rows carry the execution name in ContainerGroupName_s, so they are
# matched on that prefix and folded back onto one bucket in the volume table.
#
# EVERY SIGNATURE MUST CARRY AN INTERPRETATION, and five of them did not until
# 2026-09-10. brief-run, the four overnight lines and server-error printed "no
# interpretation recorded" in the report — the 2026-09-08 entry claimed each one
# shipped with its meaning and the code said otherwise. A signature that names
# what it matched but not what a big number means leaves the reader exactly
# where they started; naming the number is most of the value.
#
# The four OVERNIGHT signatures were added 2026-09-08 because CLAUDE.md had been
# telling the next person to "watch .ops/prod-logs for 'overnight
# investigation complete'" while this file queried six signatures, none of
# them that one. A
# reader that does not read the signal it is documented to read is the exact
# failure this control exists to end — and it is the second time the same
# mistake has been made here (see server-error, which matched 'request error'
# and so could never have fired). VERIFY THE STRING AGAINST THE SOURCE BEFORE
# ADDING A SIGNATURE.
# And verify the SCOPE too: a signature can be spelled correctly and still be
# unmatchable because the process that emits it is outside the query's filter.
# That was the 2026-09-10 finding, and it is the same failure wearing a
# different hat.
#
# THE FIRST THING THIS READER FOUND WAS A PRODUCT DEFECT, which is the point:
# 19 42501 lines over five days, all of them `aiBudget`'s ai_usage INSERT being
# refused by its own RLS policy because the service ran on the bare pool. The
# INSERT failed loudly-but-swallowed; the SELECT beside it failed SILENTLY
# (RLS returns zero rows, no error), so `used` read 0 and the AI spend cap
# never bit. Fixed 2026-09-10 by routing both through tenantQuery.
#
# THE REPORT VERIFIES ITS OWN SCOPE. After the volume table it lists, with NO
# filter, every (app, job) pair the log table actually holds. The 2026-09-10 run
# is why: it showed no `*-sync-worker` line and no `sync-complete`, and from the
# report alone those two facts are indistinguishable — either no sync ran, or
# the worker's rows sit under a name the filter does not match. Assuming the
# first is the mistake this file has now made three times. A source present in
# that unfiltered list but missing from the volume table is a SCOPE BUG; missing
# from both means it wrote nothing.
#
# EVERY SIGNATURE ALSO NAMES THE RELATIONS IT SAW. One `take_any` example line
# is a sample, not a census: run #9's rls-write-denied example named ai_usage
# and run #10's named ai_call_log — the same 19-row bucket, two different
# tables, and the second was only visible because the arbitrary pick happened
# to land there. The report now lists the set of relations extracted across the
# whole bucket, so a signature covering several tables says so instead of
# hiding all but one behind a lucky sample.
#
# WHAT RUN #10 SETTLED: the Sources seen table listed six sources (backend,
# jobs-worker, frontend, etl, neo4j, redis) with no truncation, and NO sync
# worker among them. So the scope is not the problem and never was after
# PR #133 - the Container Apps JOB has simply not executed in seven days. The
# four phase-2 sync signals are therefore blocked on a sync happening, not on
# this reader. `source-stale` above is what will say whether that silence is
# expected.
#
# RUN #11 WENT GREEN AND SAID NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE FAILURE THIS CONTROL
# EXISTS TO STOP. The relation-extraction regex added for run #11 was written
# with `\s+`, which is not a KQL string escape, so Log Analytics rejected the
# whole signature query with a parse error. The step printed COULD NOT RUN and
# then `exit 0` — a green run, on the one execution that was supposed to answer
# whether `source-stale` appears. Two fixes: the regex now carries NO backslash
# at all (`[^a-z_]*` steps over the space, backslash and quote without naming
# them), and a signature query that cannot run now FAILS THE WORKFLOW. The
# volume and source tables are context; the signatures are the report, and a
# reader that cannot read must not look like a clean one.
#
# ANSWERED 2026-09-10, run #12: `source-stale` does NOT appear. Three
# signatures over seven days - rls-write-denied 19, brief-run 5,
# runner-active 4 - and nothing else. No enabled sync schedule exists, so the
# freshness sweep has nothing to flag and the sync-worker job correspondingly
# never runs. That is the BENIGN branch: not an outage. The sweep itself is
# demonstrably alive (brief-run fires from the same scheduler-owning process),
# which is what makes this absence readable rather than meaningless. The four
# phase-2 sync signals are therefore waiting on somebody enabling or
# triggering a sync, not on this reader and not on a fix.
#
# WHAT RUN #15 SETTLED (2026-09-19, the first 14d window). Three signatures
# over fourteen days and nothing else: rls-write-denied 19, brief-run 12,
# runner-active 5.
#   - THE RLS REFUSALS ARE CLOSED. Still exactly 19, still ending
#     2026-09-09T14:14:08, with the example line's hostname on a PRE-FIX
